WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.580
The guy typically credited with inventing
what we know as the modern novel was Miguel

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:09.950
de Cervantes with his cumbersome 800+ page
book, Don Quixote.

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:19.750
But what if I told you that the real antecedent
for the modern novel was created by … ladies.

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:24.870
Before the rise of what would become the modern
novel, there was Amatory fiction.

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:28.510
Amatory fiction was a genre of fiction that
became popular in Britain in the late 17th

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:30.810
century and early 18th century.

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:36.320
As its name implies, amatory fiction is preoccupied
with sexual love and romance.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:40.879
Most of its works were short stories, it was
dominated by women, and women were the ones

00:00:40.879 --> 00:00:43.960
responsible for sharing and promoting their
own work.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.760
There are quite a few ladies we could discuss,
but today we are going to focus on the Big

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:54.190
Three, the literary lady squad that was dubbed
"the fair triumvirate of wit" made up of:

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:58.769
Eliza Haywood, Delarivier Manley, Aphra Behn.

00:00:58.769 --> 00:01:03.139
Clara Reeve, an English gothic novelist, wrote
about the history of prose fiction in The

00:01:03.139 --> 00:01:08.610
Progress of Romance (1785) and mentions with
some shade “the French and Spanish Romance,

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:12.700
and the writings of Mrs. Behn, Mrs Manly,
and Mrs. Heywood.”

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:17.220
So even as early as the 18th century as people
were documenting who was crafting the trajectory

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:20.540
of the written word, women were very much
being noted.

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:23.860
What makes amatory fiction so interesting,
and probably why it has been forgotten to

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:30.390
time, was that these were stories about women,
by women that explored their sexuality, their

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:34.440
pleasures, and the limits of autonomy they
were allowed to have in their lives.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:38.640
Before the Puritanical era of the Victorian
prudes and just general haters came along.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:44.230
In the Typical ruined woman lady plot: young
innocent woman ends up meeting a handsome,

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:49.640
probably dark haired man who never smiles
and only ever grins or smirk.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:56.020
She is seduced and later on deceived by dark
haired man and ends up ruinedddd for having

00:01:56.020 --> 00:02:00.850
sex outside of wedlock--reinforcing the notion
that women’s value is based around their

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:04.240
virginity and purity and it can be taken from
them.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:08.530
Then she had to throw herself off the cliff
because now she has nothing to offer the world.

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:14.380
Well, Amatory fiction asked: “but what if
she just liked sex and didn’t die?”

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:15.450
… some of the time...

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:19.120
Amatory fiction was considered immoral by
contemporary standards, because it allowed

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:24.870
women to have *scandalous* love affairs without
being "punished" because women are not allowed

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:26.880
to feel pleasure without consequences.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:30.980
The contemporary dismissal is part of the
reason why these stories and the authors involved

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:34.600
in them were not given their due credit for
the rise of the novel.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:39.440
So much so that later 18th century female
writers like Frances Burney really wanted

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:42.730
to separate themselves from the raunchy concept
of female authorship.

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:48.370
They weren’t like other female writers,
they were cool writers who wrote hyper realistic

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:53.540
stories about women on the cusp of being rich
if they could just marry wealthy.

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:55.010
Relatable every day content.

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:58.760
In "Novel," "Romance," and Popular Fiction
in the First Half of the Eighteenth Century

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:03.900
by Dieter Schulz, Schulz argues that the works
of the three big people you might have heard

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:04.900
of!!

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:11.990
Daniel Defoe (Robinson Crusoe), Samuel Richardson
(Clarissa), and Henry Fielding (Tom Jones),

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:16.849
should “be viewed as a reaction against
the kind of romanticized novel or novella

00:03:16.849 --> 00:03:22.660
produced by such writers as Aphra Behn, [Delarivier]
Manley, and Eliza Haywood, rather than as

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:26.959
an alternative to the tradition of "high"
romance presented by the heroic romances of

00:03:26.959 --> 00:03:28.210
the seventeenth century.”

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:29.210
Haters.

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:34.110
Daniel Defoe with his 1722 novel Moll Flanders
made sure to set up his stories as “private

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:38.150
histories” that were telling a more realistic
weighty story and were not “idle.”

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:41.760
Aka, not about fun hot girl summer things.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:45.930
Even back then male critics were very glad
to dismiss the concept of a happily ever after

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:47.709
as “fluff.”

00:03:47.709 --> 00:03:52.960
All three members of the wit squad were widely
read and Haywood’s Love in Excess was, after

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:56.970
Gulliver’s Travels and Robinson Crusoe,
one of the most popular works of fiction of

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:57.970
the era.

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:02.110
Yet we still read those other two works (mostly
abridged, but still).

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:09.470
So, it’s 1792 and Mary Wollstonecraft has
published A Vindication of the Rights of Woman

00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:11.610
and dudes are salty.

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:16.239
Male critics already think that women who
have succeeded in literature are because men

00:04:16.239 --> 00:04:18.579
have given them permission.

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:23.210
As Jane Spencer explains, “The public reception
of [Wollstonecraft's] The Rights of Woman

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:27.300
plainly demonstrates that women writers had
been accepted only on condition that they

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:32.301
remained within a limited, feminine sphere,
and did not disturb men's dominance of the

00:04:32.301 --> 00:04:34.320
'real' world outside it.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:38.180
'Women we have often placed near the throne
of literature', wrote the Critical reviewer

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:43.120
of Wollstonecraft's work: but 'if they seize
it, forgetful of our fondness, we can hurl

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:45.710
them from it'.”

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:50.180
And the way most women worked this power play
was by working satire and political commentary

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:51.590
into their works.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:54.340
What were some of these saucy stories about?

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:56.530
Well let’s see.

00:04:56.530 --> 00:05:02.630
One of Aphra Behn’s most scandalous novels
was History of the Nun or The Fair Vow Breaker:

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:07.031
This Behn novel is about a young woman named
Isabella who is the daughter of a count, who

00:05:07.031 --> 00:05:09.580
is sent to a nunnery when her mother dies.

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:13.930
She ends up falling in love with this guy
named Henault, breaks her vows as nun, and

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:17.130
commits some petty theft which gets her husband
disowned.

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:21.490
He eventually joins the army and ends up presumed
dead.

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:27.150
She ends up marrying a rich dude named Villenoys,
who was in love with her from way back when.

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:31.610
However, Henault ends up coming back and Isabella
smothers him to death and disposes of the

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:35.610
body because she is afraid of being shamed
for commiting bigamy.

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:38.990
Girl, murder is worse.

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:44.240
Isabella has such a good reputation, she almost
gets away with it, but ends up confessing

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:45.960
and is executed.

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:50.360
Before she is put to death, she gives an impassioned
speech that makes everyone go: “you know,

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:53.470
she’s pretty a cool lady, sucks she has
to die.”

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:58.530
Fantomina, or Love in a Maze by Eliza Haywood
is about this nameless high brow woman who

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:02.650
ends up having a dalliance with a man after
a night at the theater.

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:09.480
The man, called Beauplaisir, ends
up getting bored and doing the 1725 version

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:10.480
of ghosting.

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:16.389
In response, the protagonist dons three different
disguises to keep seducing Beauplaisir until

00:06:16.389 --> 00:06:18.620
they both end up getting bored with each other.

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:22.710
The story ends with our protagonist eventually
getting pregnant and being forced by her mother

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:25.010
to confess all this to Beauplaisir.

00:06:25.010 --> 00:06:29.479
Beauplaisir offers marriage and her response
is: No.

00:06:29.479 --> 00:06:32.270
Our heroine gets sent off to a French nunnery.

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:37.870
Meanwhile Beauplaisir has to deal with the
fact that he is very, very, very gullible.

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:41.690
Kathleen Lubey in “Eliza Haywood’s Amatory
Aesthetic” writes.

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:46.660
Haywood chooses amatory content not only as
a call to a sexually attuned audience, but

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:50.900
as a mode of immersing her readers [...] in
states of extreme aesthetic engagement that

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:55.150
acquaint them with human experience with an
intensity no other material can.

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:59.940
Far from being a degraded form of sensationalist
writing, amatory fiction contains the most

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:04.320
instructive potential for eliciting readers'
affect and calling their attention to the

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:05.930
implications of that affect.

00:07:05.930 --> 00:07:10.140
These stories all have different endings,
but all of them show women struggling in a

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:14.520
system that very much wants to be passive
actors in their own story.

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:17.830
They seek love, romance, sex, and adventure.

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:22.220
Each of these texts are on the heroine’s
side and even if they were forced to have

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:27.660
non-radical endings at times, the fact that
it gives voice to these desires is radical.

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:30.580
English scholar Toni O'Shaughnessy Bowers
explained:

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:35.419
“Behn, Manley, Haywood, and their peers
were writing for women increasingly cut off

00:07:35.419 --> 00:07:37.889
from the world outside the domestic circle.

00:07:37.889 --> 00:07:42.460
That world was, they knew, full of traps laid
especially for them; even the smallest error

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:45.580
could result in permanent social alienation.

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:50.410
Under these circumstances, amatory fiction
provided Augustan women with a sense of involvement

00:07:50.410 --> 00:07:54.740
in the outside world--which, for all its dangers
and disappointments, had great advantages

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:59.520
over restrictive domesticity--while allowing
them to maintain a safe distance from it.”

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:03.330
Even marriage, in these books, is treated
as something a woman has to want, rather than

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:08.400
a reward for good behavior as it is in Samuel
Richardson’s Pamela or The History of Sir

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:09.759
Charles Grandison.

00:08:09.759 --> 00:08:16.160
In 1753, Mary Wortley Montagu remarked, “Richardson
is so eager for the multiplication of [marriages]

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:22.479
I suppose he is some parish curate, whose
chief profit depends on weddings and christenings.”

00:08:22.479 --> 00:08:26.789
For those who are not aware that is a soft
drag (it is not a read).

00:08:26.789 --> 00:08:30.180
But these women weren’t just writing lust
tales for the sake of it.

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:32.419
Interwoven was heavy political satire.

00:08:32.419 --> 00:08:37.680
In The New Atalantis, an influential political
satire by Delarivier Manley, which have the

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:44.740
goddesses Justice and Virtue on earth witnesses
all kinds of human nastiness: orgies, seductions,

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:46.380
and assault.

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:47.380
Just terrible.

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:53.020
It mocked several members of the Whigg party
including Sarah Churchill, who was then a

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:55.860
favorite of the Queen.

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:59.750
In fact the scandal and public fever around
Manley’s writing was particularly impactful

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:04.660
in damaging the relationship between Sarah
Churchill and Queen Anne, although they decided

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:07.330
not to include that in The Favourite for some
reason.

00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:11.290
Manley ended up being arrested, but she was
adamant that the figures in the book were

00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:17.230
totally fictional and eventually charges were
dropped so it couldn’t be libel so there.

00:09:17.230 --> 00:09:20.360
Professor of English, Catherine Gallagher,
explains of Manley’s work:

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:24.740
“The “allegorical style” favored by
Manley, for example, involved telling scandalous

00:09:24.740 --> 00:09:30.140
stories about recognizable real persons, with
the name and some circumstances altered.

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:34.240
The stories were given distant historical,
exotic, or mythical settings and were often

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:37.740
interspersed with stories lacking apparent
referents…

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:42.500
What Manley gained from the allegorical necessity
of altering and therefore inventing circumstances

00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:46.680
was a representational density that enabled
especially effective libel.

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:51.950
Manley’s talent was for crafting the invented
circumstances into what we would now call

00:09:51.950 --> 00:09:58.010
a “novelistically” satisfying story, one
read for its own sake as well as for its scandalous

00:09:58.010 --> 00:09:59.140
referentiality.”

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:04.770
If these women were so impactful and interesting,
to the point where they ruined semi-gay royal

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:07.590
relationships, why aren’t they more well
known?

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:10.680
Well, it was because they were raunchy.

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:14.850
Other female authors of the time didn’t
want to be associated with the fair triumvirate

00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:18.640
who were also called “prostitutes of the
pen.”

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:20.830
William Warner, an English essayist wrote:

00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:24.970
“The incorporation of the novel of amorous
intrigue within the elevated novel of the

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:31.730
1740s--in, for example, Pamela, Joseph Andrews,
Clarissa, and Tom Jones--is one of the means

00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:34.970
by which old pleasures are disowned and forgotten.

00:10:34.970 --> 00:10:39.981
In their novels of the 1740s, Richardson and
Fielding promote this “forgetting,” first

00:10:39.981 --> 00:10:44.420
by defacing the novel of amorous intrigue,
and then by providing their own novels as

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:48.680
replacements for novels they characterize
as degraded and immoral.

00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:53.899
These new novels overwrite--by disavowing
but appropriating, tossing out but recycling--the

00:10:53.899 --> 00:10:55.470
novels they spurn…”

00:10:55.470 --> 00:11:00.040
It wasn’t until early feminists and other
female authors like Virginia Woolf rediscovered

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:03.149
them that history started giving these ladies
their due.

00:11:03.149 --> 00:11:07.040
The works of these women are being studied
more, but due to the racey subject matter

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:10.810
most people ever hear of these women in college
or graduate school.

00:11:10.810 --> 00:11:14.750
Despite the work they did to help develop
what we now know of the English novel, they

00:11:14.750 --> 00:11:20.060
are still under loved and under appreciated,
much like Amy Adams at the Oscars.

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:25.399
When we talk about female authorship we can
always pull out the big guns: Brontes, Austen,

00:11:25.399 --> 00:11:31.940
Alcott, Woolf, and James, but that is such
a small cafeteria table, when the reality

00:11:31.940 --> 00:11:36.510
is that there was a whole school of female
writers who not only wrote, but did it well

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:38.279
enough to set the standard.

00:11:38.279 --> 00:11:42.740
To quote Virginia Woolf: "All women together
ought to let flowers fall upon the tomb of

00:11:42.740 --> 00:11:48.690
Aphra Behn which is, most scandalously but
rather appropriately, in Westminster Abbey,

00:11:48.690 --> 00:12:00.699
for it was she who earned them the right to
speak their minds.”

